        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "02537897-db67-5154-b278-72befb4a3517",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Onesimus, Cotton Mather\n- Places: Boston, Salem\n- Concepts: slavery, inoculation, disease prevention, smallpox outbreak, hybridization experiments, Salem witch trials\n\nFacts:\n- Onesimus was an African-born man held as a slave by Puritan minister Cotton Mather in Boston in the late 1600s and 1700s.\n- Onesimus helped mitigate the impact of a smallpox outbreak in Boston by introducing Cotton Mather to the principle of inoculation.\n- Cotton Mather promoted inoculation for disease prevention and is remembered for a scientific legacy that also included hybridization experiments.\n- Cotton Mather is most frequently remembered today for his vigorous support for the Salem witch trials.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
